          "A foreign corporation doing business in the US is taxed only on income effectively connected with a US trade or business (ECI), determined under domestic law by a facts-and-circumstances trade-or-business test, net of deductions at ordinary corporate rates — but where a US income tax treaty applies, the treaty typically raises that bar considerably, taxing business profits only if the foreign enterprise has a permanent establishment (PE) in the US, meaning a fixed place of business through which the enterprise's business is wholly or partly carried on, or a dependent agent habitually concluding contracts on its behalf. Separately, and regardless of PE or trade-or-business status, US-source Fixed, Determinable, Annual, or Periodical income (FDAP) — dividends, interest, rents, royalties paid to a foreign person — is subject to a flat 30 percent withholding tax at the source under IRC §§1441 and 1442, collected by the US payor, unless a treaty reduces the rate (commonly to 0, 5, or 15 percent) and the foreign recipient properly certifies treaty eligibility on Form W-8BEN or W-8BEN-E.",
