    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one map of how US tax law actually works end to end. Jurisdiction and entity classification answer the threshold questions of who can be taxed at all and through what vehicle, and gross income and capital-gain character then determine how much of what that vehicle earns is actually reached, and at what rate. Transfer pricing, permanent establishment and FDAP withholding, and the foreign tax credit and treaty network extend that same domestic framework across a border, first asking how income gets allocated among related entities, then how a foreign business's US presence gets taxed (or not), then how double taxation of the same income gets relieved once both sides have taxed it. Economic substance and the evasion/avoidance line then mark the outer boundary of what the law will tolerate in minimizing any of the above, one through a civil penalty regime for contrived transactions, the other through genuinely criminal exposure for willful concealment. We close with limitations and forum choice, because none of the substantive analysis in this lecture matters if the year in question is already closed, or if the wrong procedural path was chosen to contest an adjustment. Notice how often a single fact pattern implicates several of these doctrines at once: a cross-border licensing arrangement can raise transfer pricing, withholding, treaty-PE, and economic-substance questions simultaneously, which is exactly why real tax practice is rarely about applying one rule in isolation, but about working out which of several overlapping regimes actually governs, and in what order.",
