          "The Skatteförfarandelagen requires that any taxpayer disagreeing with a Skatteverket decision must first request omprövning, reconsideration, from Skatteverket itself, generally within six years from the end of the relevant tax year for most matters (a notably longer general period than Germany's four years or Spain's four years), before any appeal to court becomes available at all; only after Skatteverket has ruled on the omprövning request (or failed to act within statutory time limits) can the taxpayer appeal, överklaga, to Förvaltningsrätten, the administrative court of first instance, with further appeal available to Kammarrätten, the administrative court of appeal, and ultimately Högsta förvaltningsdomstolen, the Supreme Administrative Court, as the final instance, generally requiring leave to appeal (prövningstillstånd) at both appellate stages. Skatteverket's own power to reassess a closed year on an eftertaxering, after-tax-assessment, basis is more tightly time-limited than the taxpayer's own six-year reconsideration window: generally within six years of the relevant tax year for ordinary reassessment, but the agency can reach back further, without the ordinary time limit applying at all, where the taxpayer's own conduct involved providing deliberately false information or failing to disclose required facts, a fraud-adjacent extension conceptually parallel to the German, French, and Spanish extended periods covered earlier in this lecture.",
